# Spectrum Studio upgrades from main

This rule applies only to sessions doing Spectrum Studio work. It is an
agent instruction, not a Git hook or background automation.

## Check at session startup

At the beginning of Studio work in each new session, before editing:

1. Read `docs/spectrum-studio/AGENTS.md`. Inspect the current branch, working
   tree, and whether a merge, rebase, or other sequenced Git operation is active.
   Use Git commands, not filesystem searches through `.git`. If HEAD is
   detached or an operation is active, report that state and defer the comparison.
2. Verify that `origin` identifies `adobe/spectrum-design-data` on GitHub.
   Do not substitute another remote, repository, or target branch. If the
   configuration differs or is ambiguous, ask the user before fetching.
3. Fetch main once, from the repository root:

   ```bash
   git fetch --no-tags --no-recurse-submodules origin 'refs/heads/main:refs/remotes/origin/main'
   ```

   This is permission to refresh the remote-tracking ref, not to change the
   working branch or files. Do not force a rejected fetch or prune refs.
4. Record the current branch, HEAD commit and fetched `origin/main` commit
   in the session context. Check for shallow/incomplete history and a common
   ancestor before interpreting the comparison:

   ```bash
   git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository
   git merge-base HEAD refs/remotes/origin/main
   git rev-list --left-right --count HEAD...refs/remotes/origin/main
   ```

   The first count is commits unique to the working branch; the second is
   main commits missing from the working branch. A branch can be ahead and
   behind at the same time.
   A zero second count means it contains the fetched main commit, not necessarily
   that it matches main exactly.
5. Report briefly: branch, missing-main count, branch-only count, and any
   limitation. If behind, name `upgrade-studio-from-main` as the available
   workflow. A dirty checkout still permits this fetch-and-report check, but
   blocks an upgrade.

If fetch fails, the ref is missing, history is incomplete, or no common
ancestor can be established, report that the check is unavailable or uncertain.
Never describe a cached result as a fresh check or interpret an error as zero
missing commits. Do not deepen history or repair Git configuration automatically.
These warnings do not block unrelated Studio work.

Do not repeat the same check or notice each turn. Recheck when the branch
changes or before a requested upgrade; refresh it after a resumed session if
its branch or recorded commits no longer match.

## Upgrade only on request

Read and follow
[upgrade-studio-from-main](../skills/upgrade-studio-from-main/SKILL.md) when the
user requests an upgrade or rebase from main for Studio.

* A startup warning is not permission to rebase.
* A requested upgrade permits a rebase after the skill's safety checks and
  small, tested compatibility fixes that preserve documented behavior.
* Pause for every Git conflict, data migration, rule change, behavior or
  architecture decision, or uncertain failure. Use a session plan and Copilot's
  native approval prompt when changes need a plan.
* Never stash, discard work, commit, push, force push, or perform a destructive
  rollback automatically. This workflow does not authorize those actions,
  including through generic session-completion instructions.
